# Introduction to Johnston Engineering

Johnston Engineering (JE) is a mechanical engineering firm located in Spokane, Washington **specializing in the design, analysis, integration, ****build****, and testing of first-of-a-kind systems**. In operation since 2015, JE brings over 250 years of combined engineering experience across a team of 23, with deep expertise in system integration and advanced engineering solutions. Our work spans critical material recovery systems, industrial machinery, and biotechnology, as well as clean technology sectors including carbon capture, hydrogen, nuclear systems, and long-duration energy storage. 

A defining advantage of Johnston Engineering is our **Analysis-Driven Design** approach. At JE, our designers are also analysts, utilizing physics-based simulations of thermal, structural, and fluid-flow analysis to optimize and validate designs before we build systems. This closely integrated coupling of design and analysis ensures that performance is validated before fabrication begins, preventing part failures and accelerating development. Using SolidWorks 3D CAD and ANSYS Finite Element Analysis (FEA) and Computational Fluid Dynamics (CFD), our team excels in component modeling, full system integration, and simulation of forces, pressures, torques, heat transfer, and complex flow interactions. We iterate through design and analysis cycles until the system is fully optimized for performance, modularity, reliability, serviceability and manufacturability. This helps us **develop the system faster and with less risk.** 

A second core strength is that **our designers are also your builders**. The same engineers who model and analyze the system are also in charge of fabrication oversight, procurement, assembly, and testing. This continuity produces engineers with a uniquely deep understanding of the design intent, system behavior, and how the system is installed in the real world. This results in higher-quality, better-engineered hardware with fewer redesign cycles.

Third, we focus specifically on **first-of-a-kind (FOAK) development**. JE exists to reduce risk when scaling technologies. We have deep experience with technologies in the **TRL 3–****9** range and regularly support clients transitioning concepts into integrated subsystems, pilot systems, and field-deployable hardware.

Our team also has experience designing systems for **critical mineral recovery**, including novel absorption and desorption technologies, complex thermal and fluid environments, and rare earth/critical mineral separation processes from benchtop through field-ready units of similar size to the Dual Trailer system. This includes systems operating under tightly controlled temperatures, pressures, flow rates, and reactive conditions essential for high-purity material recovery.

JE President, Andy Johnston, P.E., leads the team at JE and provides subject-matter expertise in structural, thermal, and fluid flow analysis, with over 20 years of experience in advanced thermal-management systems, energy technologies, aerospace, and defense. Before founding Johnston Engineering, Andy spent 11 years at Parker Aerospace Thermal Management Systems designing liquid heating, cooling, and environmental control systems for military and aerospace platforms. 

To date, JE has completed more than 190 first-of-a-kind engineering projects across the U.S. Department of Energy, and private industry. **F****ive**** of Johnston Engnieering’s previous project examples have been included in this proposal ****in**** ****Section 2 below****. **

Our focus on FOAK development, combined with our design-analysis-build-test approach, makes Johnston Engineering an ideal partner to develop KMX’s Dual Trailer system.

# Johnston Engineering Project Examples

##  Industrial-Scale Electrolyzer Pilot Plant

Johnston Engineering partnered with OCOchem to support the scale-up and deployment of their industrial-scale CO₂ electrolyzer technology. The project has now culminated in the successful commissioning of the world’s first pilot plant using multiple industrial-scale cells to directly convert CO₂ and water into organic molecules.

**Project ****Goal:** Scale up the CO2 electrolyzer cell size by 10x

**Johnston Engineering Provided Services: **Johnston Engineering provides comprehensive mechanical design, analysis, building, and test services to ensure the successful development and testing of the industrial-scale CO2 electrolyzer cell.

- Mechanical Design:

- Development of flow visualization models to optimize the internal flow dynamics.

- Design of mounting and work platforms to support the electrolyzer and ensure safe operation.

- Custom hardware design for power supply systems to meet the unique requirements of the scaled-up electrolyzer.

- Computational Fluid Dynamics (CFD):

- **Pressure drop analysis** to ensure efficient fluid flow and minimize energy losses.

- **Flow distribution analysis** to achieve uniform flow and optimal performance across the electrolyzer cell.

- Structural Analysis

- Stress analysis of the electrolyzer mounting frame and work platforms. 

- Hazardous Material Control Area:

- **Design and build of a dedicated hazardous material control area** for safe hydrogen testing.

- Implementation of ventilation, containment, and operational safety measures for H₂ handling and testing

## Containerized Electrochemical System Design

Johnston Engineering is supporting Lectrolyst in the development and commercialization of its modular electrochemical system designed to convert CO₂ and renewable electricity into sustainable chemicals. Lectrolyst’s technology leverages advanced catalyst and cell designs to achieve high-efficiency electrochemical conversion, and the current project focuses on validating, optimizing, and preparing the system for containerized pilot deployment. With modeling, analysis, and containerized system design completed, the project is now prepared to move into **fabrication and full system build**. Johnston Engineering is ready to support Lectrolyst through the build phase, including procurement support, mechanical build services, assembly oversight, and commissioning support.

**Project Goals:**

- Evaluate and optimize the performance of Lectrolyst’s electrolyzer stack architecture.

- Perform mechanical and structural validation of cell compression, sealing, and operational loads.

- Package the full system into a 40-ft containerized pilot-scale platform suitable for fabrication and field deployment.

- Identify and cost the bill of materials (BOM) for the first build unit, enabling Lectrolyst to move directly into construction.

**Johnston Engineering Provided Services: **

- CFD Modeling & Electrolyzer Performance Analysis

- Conducted CFD modeling of the electrolyzer cell and full-stack flow pathways.

- Analyzed flow distribution, pressure gradients, and reactant delivery uniformity to identify opportunities for improved efficiency and reduced parasitic losses.

- Performed one to two optimized geometry iterations to refine internal flow features and enhance performance at the stack level.

- Structural Analysis

- Performed structural and compression analysis of the electrolyzer stack assembly.

- Evaluated clamping forces, gasket behavior, and mechanical tolerances to ensure proper sealing, structural integrity, and safe long-duration operation.

- System Packaging & Mechanical Integration

- Developed the complete system packaging layout within a standard 40-ft shipping container, including:

- Equipment arrangement and envelope management

- Maintenance access, operator pathways, and safety clearances

- Integration of auxiliary systems, power components, and balance-of-plant hardware

- Ensured the design supports modular, field-deployable operation.

- 4. Bill of Materials (BOM) Development & Costing

- Identified the required components and subsystems for the first full build.

- Developed and priced a detailed BOM, enabling Lectrolyst to budget, schedule procurement, and prepare for fabrication.

## Containerized Power Generation Racking System

Figure 1 Containerized power generation racking system deployed September 2024

Johnston Engineering collaborated with our client to develop a containerized power generation system equipped with a custom racking system. This project involves customizing shipping containers, equipped with a custom racking system to secure power generation equipment in transportation and in use once on-site as shown in Figure 1. The system was designed to ensure robust structural integrity, particularly under transportation loads, while maintaining efficient and compact packaging, as the complete system will utilize hundreds of containers stacked vertically and back-to-back.  

**Client Goal:**

- Develop a scalable containerized system for power generation.

- Ensure structural integrity and durability during transportation and operation.

**Johnston Engineering Services Provided:**

Johnston Engineering provided comprehensive design, analysis, fabrication and integration services for the containerized power generation system, ensuring the successful execution of the project and the client's objectives.

- Design:

- Mechanical design of the racking system, including custom components to secure power generation equipment within the container.

- Mechanical design of the electrical devices that reside in the racking system.  

- Development of detailed 3D CAD models and assembly drawings for precise fabrication and integration.

- Structural Analysis:

- Analysis of structural frames to ensure stability and integrity during transportation and operation.

- Detailed stress and load analysis to verify the strength and durability of the frames and components, ensuring they could withstand the transportation acceleration loads.

- Build and Assembly:

- Fabrication and assembly of the entire system inside a 20ft shipping container, ensuring compact and efficient packaging on-site at Johnston Engineering.

- Quoting, purchasing, and inspection of all externally fabricated components from vendors to meet project specifications.

- Coordination of all fabrication and subcontractor activities to ensure the project was completed on time and within budget.

This project was completed in September 2024. The above picture showcases the initial assembly stage inside the 20ft container.

